Z||000024||BN Boomer||10-16-2001||09:08 AM||||If you plan on doing much towing, you really need a more appropriate tow vehicle. The Cherokee's light duty drivetrain and brakes along with it's short wheelbase and narrow track make it unsuitable for towing much beyond a small utility, boat, or motorcycle trailer. I would truly hate too see how a Cherokee would handle a trailer of that size in a crosswind.<P>Most rental places around my neck of the woods wouldn't even rent you that trailer to tow behind a Cherokee.<P>You could buy an older 3/4 ton pickup and a steel trailer for the price of that aluminum one.||||reg||1
Z||000025||bHooper||10-16-2001||09:20 AM||||i was going to suggest something similar.  (i understand why you like the cherokee, if they had 3" more legroom in the back seat, they would be perfect vehicles!).<P>perhaps you can sell the jeep, take the $2500 difference on the price of the trailer and go to the dealership auction and buy a really nice late model 8 cylinder sport ute?  or keep the jeep and buy an '89 full sized Bronco (with the 5.7 liter engine).  <P>they are awesome towing vehicles.<BR><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B>Red</B></FONT></FONT> 86 GT 5 speed<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><B>Black</B></FONT> <u>86 SE Automatic</u>||||reg||1
Z||000026||thomas_l||10-16-2001||10:25 AM||||Yikes! that's a huge price for an open trailer. I have been prowling for a car trailer too, but I have enough truck (6400# total tow capacity) for a steel trailer.<P>I did see this one tho:  <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=596765311" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=596765311</A>  <P>A nice looking aluminum (open) trailer with a protective shield up front. He has his opening price OK but who knows where the reserve is. I think I'll just hold on to a few $1000 for future gasoline purchases towing a heavier trailer  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||11
